  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1020 PM MDT Sun Oct 25 2026\n\nShowers have become more isolated this evening as the low \npressure system lifts northeast across northwest Colorado and\nexits to the northeast. Hi-res CAMs indicate most activity \nwinding down by midnight and moving onto the Front Range east of\nthe Divide by Monday morning. We could however still see some \nlingering orographic showers over the northern and central \nDivide Monday morning with light snow. Very little snow \naccumulation is expected but conditions could be slick in the \nmorning over the passes.\n\nDRIER PATTERN MONDAY THROUGH THURSDAY:\n\nMuch drier air moves in Monday and lasts through at least mid \nweek as a shortwave high pressure ridge slides overhead Monday. \nA couple shortwave troughs will push across the Intermountain \nWest from the PacNW Monday night through Tuesday night, \nresulting in a breezy southwest flow Tuesday afternoon and some \nlight showers possible over the northern mountains. Overall \nconditions remain dry with little in the way of precipitation \nthrough mid week as temperatures hover around 5 degrees below \nnormal with a warmup towards normal or slightly above by \nThursday. \n\nFRIDAY INTO NEXT WEEKEND:\n\nSouthwest flow will begin to advect some moisture off the \nPacific late Thursday into Friday, resulting in increased cloud \ncover. Models are not in the best agreement beyond Friday with \nregards to how to handle this influx of Pacific moisture but \nshowers are possible Friday afternoon, particularly over the \nsouthern and central Divide as a shortwave trough skirts the \nFront Range from the southwest. A deeper trough moves into the \nwest Sunday into early next week but the jury is out on what \nthis means for our weather. While conditions look to turn more \nunsettled with increasing moisture from the Pacific, there are \ndiscrepancies between models and model runs on timing, how cold \nit might get, and the duration of said moisture as some of the \nlatest model runs are showing a more progressive solution than \nearlier runs. This is still a ways out but due to poor model \nagreement and poor model consistency, will hold off on \ndiscussing too much detail yet. This is the next system to \nwatch, which could result in a return to cooler and unsettled \nconditions with potential for mountain snowfall.",
